{0 ModelKit}

ModelKit is a portable OCaml library for cohesive classical machine learning
workflows.

The current development version adds immutable dense {!Modelkit.Dataset}
admission, explicit feature-finiteness policies, stable schema fingerprints,
zero-copy row-index views, and copy/view reports to the existing opaque
float64 {!Modelkit.Vector} and {!Modelkit.Matrix} values. Public specification,
estimator, transformer, scorer,
splitter, execution, RNG, and numerical-backend module types define the
extension boundaries with separate unfitted and fitted states. The portable
runtime includes fixed-order compensated numerical kernels, sequential
stable-order execution, deterministic logical seed derivation, and a pure
SplitMix64 random-number stream. Estimators and end-to-end workflows will
arrive in later releases. Portable tests consume committed scikit-learn
reference data without requiring Python during a normal build or test run.

{1 Executable example}

Dataset admission permits NaN missing markers only when explicitly requested:

{@ocaml[
# open Modelkit;;
# let x = Matrix.of_arrays [| [| 1.; Float.nan |]; [| 3.; 4. |] |] |> Result.get_ok;;
val x : Matrix.t = <abstr>
# let y = Target.classification [| 0; 1 |];;
val y : Target.classification Target.t = <abstr>
# let dataset = Dataset.create ~finiteness:Dataset.Allow_nan ~x ~y () |> Result.get_ok;;
val dataset : Target.classification Dataset.t = <abstr>
# Dataset.sample_count dataset, Dataset.feature_count dataset;;
- : int * int = (2, 2)
]}

The reference backend preserves small terms that ordinary floating-point
summation can lose to cancellation:

{@ocaml[
# open Modelkit;;
# Reference_backend.sum (Vector.of_array [| 1e16; 1.0; -1e16 |]);;
- : float = 1.
]}
